from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "7. First Corinthians 12: The Gifts of Healing"),
("p", "Claim: the clause “gifts of healing” stands twice in 1 Corinthians 12, once in each list of gifts and once in a question, and the chapter says three things about it: it is given by the Spirit as He wills, it is one of several gifts, and not all have it. The chapter does not instruct any assembly to seek it or to exercise it, and it does not say how long it continues. Volume 2 reads the two lists of the chapter in order, and Pauline Doctrine Series Volume 4 reads the chapter for the order of the assembly. This chapter takes only the healing clause."),
("sub", "7.1 To whom the letter is written"),
("p", "The letter opens with its address. “Paul, called to be an apostle of Jesus Christ through the will of God, and Sosthenes our brother, Unto the church of God which is at Corinth, to them that are sanctified in Christ Jesus, called to be saints, with all that in every place call upon the name of Jesus Christ our Lord, both theirs and ours” (1 Corinthians 1:1, 2). The letter is to the church at Corinth, and the address also includes “all that in every place call upon the name of Jesus Christ our Lord.” The volume does not rest its ruling on a narrow reading of this address. The ruling rests on the program in which the gifts were given and on the end of that program, which chapter 12 of this volume argues. The address shows that the letter belongs to Paul’s ministry, and the gifts it names are those he describes in Corinth."),
("sub", "7.2 The first list"),
("p", "Paul begins with the question: “Now concerning spiritual gifts, brethren, I would not have you ignorant” (1 Corinthians 12:1). He says that “there are diversities of gifts, but the same Spirit. And there are differences of administrations, but the same Lord. And there are diversities of operations, but it is the same God which worketh all in all” (1 Corinthians 12:4–6). The manifestation of the Spirit “is given to every man to profit withal” (1 Corinthians 12:7). The list follows: “For to one is given by the Spirit the word of wisdom; to another the word of knowledge by the same Spirit; To another faith by the same Spirit; to another the gifts of healing by the same Spirit” (1 Corinthians 12:8, 9)."),
("p", "The gifts of healing stand fourth in the list, after the word of wisdom, the word of knowledge and faith, and before miracles, prophecy, discerning of spirits, tongues and interpretation. The list ends: “But all these worketh that one and the selfsame Spirit, dividing to every man severally as he will” (1 Corinthians 12:11). The gift is given by the Spirit, to one and not to another, and it is given as He wills. The sentence does not state that any believer may ask for it, and it does not state that the will of the Spirit is the same in every assembly."),
("sub", "7.3 The body and its members"),
("p", "The list is followed by the figure of the body. “For as the body is one, and hath many members, and all the members of that one body, being many, are one body: so also is Christ. For by one Spirit are we all baptized into one body, whether we be Jews or Gentiles, whether we be bond or free” (1 Corinthians 12:12, 13). The figure is the ground for the diversity: “But now hath God set the members every one of them in the body, as it hath pleased him” (1 Corinthians 12:18). It also speaks of the members’ care for each other: “And whether one member suffer, all the members suffer with it; or one member be honoured, all the members rejoice with it” (1 Corinthians 12:26). The sentence names suffering in the body and says that the members suffer with it. It does not say that the suffering is healed by a gift."),
("sub", "7.4 The second list"),
("p", "The second list is in the setting of the church and has an order: “And God hath set some in the church, first apostles, secondarily prophets, thirdly teachers, after that miracles, then gifts of healings, helps, governments, diversities of tongues” (1 Corinthians 12:28). The gifts of healings stand after miracles and before helps and governments. The list is of what God has “set” in the church, and it sets beside one another gifts that Scripture elsewhere treats as signs and gifts that are not signs, such as helps and governments. The volume reports the order and does not rank the gifts further."),
("sub", "7.5 The questions"),
("p", "Paul then asks seven questions in a row: “Are all apostles? are all prophets? are all teachers? are all workers of miracles? Have all the gifts of healing? do all speak with tongues? do all interpret?” (1 Corinthians 12:29, 30). The form of each question expects the answer no. The gifts of healing are given to some and not to others. The chapter closes: “But covet earnestly the best gifts: and yet shew I unto you a more excellent way” (1 Corinthians 12:31). Volume 2 reads the verse in chapter 5."),
("sub", "7.6 What the chapter does not say"),
("p", "The chapter says what the gift is, where it stands and that it is not given to all. It does not say that any believer in any assembly is to seek it, and it does not say how a sick person in Corinth is to obtain it. It does not give a rule for its use, because the rules for the assembly come in chapter 14. It does not say how long the gifts continue. The chapter itself says only that the Spirit gives them as He wills and that God has set them in the church."),
("p", "The volume’s ruling is that the gifts of healing belong to the Corinthian overlap and to the sign-program of that period, and that they end with it. The chapter does not state that ending, and the volume does not say that it does. The inference runs from the sign-program’s end at Acts 28, argued in Volume 1 and Volume 2, to the gifts that served it. Healing ends programmatically at Acts 28. No verse states it; the program ends, and the gift ends with it."),
("tbl", "The gifts of healing in 1 Corinthians 12.", [
    ("1 Corinthians 12:9", T("1 Cor 12:9"), "In the first list, fourth, given by the same Spirit."),
    ("1 Corinthians 12:11", T("1 Cor 12:11"), "The Spirit divides to every man severally as he will."),
    ("1 Corinthians 12:28", T("1 Cor 12:28"), "In the second list, after miracles, in the order God has set."),
    ("1 Corinthians 12:30", T("1 Cor 12:30"), "The question of the gifts of healing expects the answer no."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The gifts of healing are one gift among others, given by the Spirit to some as He wills.",
    "Paul’s questions expect the answer that not all have the gifts of healing.",
    "The chapter gives no instruction to seek the gift and gives no end to it.",
    "The ruling that the gifts end with the sign-program rests on the program and on Acts 28, and the volume says no verse states it.",
  ]),
]
